Quoting  Ray Dillinger  <bear@sonic.net>:
>True.  I wasn't counting the top one-percent of players when I said 
>"virtually" all.  There really are people for whom the above statement
>which you quoted is false.  I consider them to be more the exception 
>than the rule, however.  Most players are still on the learning curve. 

I'm nowhere near the top one percent; the problem is more insidious.
Sure, the opening game still has a lot of interest, but give me a
character in the middle game with MR, reflection, and no unexpected
problems (reasonable HP, AC, weapon) and I'll bet my chance of ascension
is close enough to 100% as makes no difference (and since I last lost a
character after the Castle in 3.2, my play record backs that up).

Sure, there's technically extra things to learn - the "stylish entrance"
is the last major play technique that comes to mind - but most of this is
putting extra endless polish on "just in case", or just saving clock time,
which there's no prizes for.

There isn't really a learning curve for the later game anymore. I think
the middle game has always been a cruise for anyone who was past the
"stupid mistakes" stage [1], much of the endgame is a cruise for any
reasonably disciplined and knowledgeable player, and although a lot of
people manage to drop the ball on Astral or in the Sanctum I am not a
particularly exceptional player for not doing so.

[1] of course some people never pass it but this is perhaps something they
must learn for roguelikes in general, not just for NetHack.
